# Deliberately no /api/* rule here. Cloudflare Pages' _redirects only accepts
# status codes 200, 301, 302, 303, 307, or 308 -- an invalid status is silently
# ignored at build time, not rejected, so there is no way to make this file
# answer /api/* with a 404 or any other "this path does not belong to the SPA"
# response. A 200-rewrite would be worse than nothing: it would make an
# unrouted /api/* request look identical to a successful API response.
# The Worker route in wrangler.toml (`viabiancadelhi.com/api/*`) is therefore
# the *only* mechanism keeping /api/* out of the catch-all below -- Cloudflare
# Routes take precedence over Pages for a matching hostname, so as long as
# that route exists and is active, this catch-all never sees an /api/*
# request at all. src/test/hosting.test.ts pins the route's existence,
# pattern and zone instead of anything in this file. If you're adding a rule
# here for some other path, know that this file cannot express "fail loudly"
# for it -- see docs/cloudflare-cutover.md's deploy-time curl smoke check for
# the only reliable way to confirm the route is actually live.
# The SPA catch-all, deliberately NOT `/*`.
#
# `/*` matched EVERY path, including `/assets/index-<hash>.css`. Cloudflare
# Pages consults this file only when it cannot serve a path as a real file --
# so during the propagation window of a deploy, a request for a brand-new
# hashed asset got index.html back with a **200**, and `_headers` marks
# `/assets/*` `max-age=31536000, immutable`, so that HTML was then cached at a
# content-hashed asset URL for a year. The filename never changes, so nothing
# ever revalidates it. Observed live: the stylesheet served as `text/html` to
# every browser (which sends an `Origin` header on subresource requests) while
# curl, which does not, got the real CSS -- and separately on two different
# favicons, on two different hosts, from one deploy. A poisoned stylesheet is
# an unstyled site; a poisoned JS bundle would be a blank page.
#
# Every real route is one segment (`/blogs`, `/catering`, and every page slug
# via App.tsx's `/:slug`) except the dashboard, so a single-segment
# placeholder covers them all and leaves `/assets/<file>` -- two segments --
# to 404 honestly instead. `/` needs no rule: index.html exists at the root
# and Pages serves it directly.
#
# The cost, stated: a nonexistent DEEP path (`/catering/nope`) now gets
# Cloudflare's own 404 instead of the site's branded NotFound page. That is a
# fair trade for a real 404 status, and single-segment misses -- the ones
# anyone actually mistypes -- still reach NotFound.
/edit/manage      /index.html   200
/edit/manage/*    /index.html   200
/:page            /index.html   200
